<BaseLayout title="Paint scratch repair">
  <h1>Paint scratch repair</h1>
  <p>This page covers assessment and repair planning for a scratch on a painted metal body panel. The reference vehicle is Denim Blue, but the exact paint code must be read from the car before selecting touch-up materials.</p>

  <h2>1. Clean and inspect</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Wash the panel with automotive shampoo and clean water, then dry it with a clean microfiber towel.</li>
    <li>Inspect in bright, indirect light from several angles.</li>
    <li>Determine whether the white mark is transferred material, a clear-coat scratch, exposed primer, or bare metal.</li>
    <li>Do not repeatedly rub a dry or dirty surface; this can add scratches.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>2. Choose the repair approach</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Appearance</th><th>Likely approach</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>White mark sits on top of the paint and transfers to a paint-safe microfiber/cleaner</td><td>Careful removal of the transfer material; re-inspect the underlying finish.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scratch is visible but does not catch a fingernail</td><td>May be in the clear coat. A paint-safe polish may improve it, but test a small area and avoid removing excessive clear coat.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fingernail catches in the scratch</td><td>May extend through the clear coat. Touch-up or professional spot repair may be more appropriate than polishing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Primer or bare metal is visible</td><td>Protect the exposed area and arrange a compatible primer/basecoat/clearcoat repair promptly to prevent corrosion.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Match the paint</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Find the factory paint code on the vehicle identification label.</li>
    <li>Order by paint code, not just the name Denim Blue.</li>
    <li>Check whether the product is intended for a metallic finish and whether it includes the required basecoat and clearcoat steps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Avoid making it worse</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not use household cleaners, abrasive pads, or aggressive compounds.</li>
    <li>Do not wet-sand unless you understand paint thickness and have the proper materials and experience.</li>
    <li>Do not apply touch-up paint over dirt, wax, or loose corrosion.</li>
    <li>Follow the paint product's temperature, curing, and application instructions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Job notes</h2>
  <p>Record the panel, scratch length and depth, paint code, products used, preparation, number of coats, and curing time. Photograph the damage before and after in the same lighting.</p>
</BaseLayout>
